```mdx
---
title: "Building SabiScore: Architecture Decisions in a Resource-Constrained Production System"
date: "2025-11-12"
summary: "How ensemble model selection, Redis caching strategy, and graceful fallback produced a reliable ML inference API under infrastructure constraints."
tags: ["ML Engineering", "FastAPI", "Production Systems", "Redis"]
featured: true
---

## The constraint was the specification

When infrastructure latency is unpredictable, every architectural decision has a cost function
that only becomes visible in production — never in a notebook.

SabiScore needed to deliver real-time predictions during peak match windows with a team of one.
That constraint shaped every decision below.

## The model selection decision

**Chosen:** Ensemble meta-learner (XGBoost + LightGBM + scikit-learn stacking)
**Rejected:** Single-model XGBoost

The ensemble produced a measurable accuracy improvement on the hold-out validation set.
The cost was inference latency — which the Redis caching layer absorbed.

## The caching architecture

**Chosen:** Redis inference result cache with TTL keyed on input feature hash
**Rejected:** Direct Postgres reads per request

During initial load testing, direct reads exceeded 200ms at peak windows.
The cache eliminated latency spikes without requiring infrastructure scaling.

## What shipped vs. what was cut

**Shipped:** Real-time feature engineering pipeline · FastAPI inference layer · Redis caching · Postgres persistence · Next.js frontend · Docker deployment

**Cut:** Drift monitoring dashboard (deferred to v2 — logging is in place, alerting is next)
